fullName":"Overall CC","lastName":"Overall","firstName":"Christopher C","initials":"CC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biological Sciences Division, Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Richland, Washington, United States of America."}]}},{"fullName":"Johnson RC","lastName":"Johnson","firstName":"Rudd C","initials":"RC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology and Immunology, Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, Oregon, United States of America."}]}},{"fullName":"Jones MB","lastName":"Jones","firstName":"Marcus B","initials":"MB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Infectious Diseases, J. Craig Venter Institute, Rockville, Maryland, United States of America."}]}},{"fullName":"McDermott JE","lastName":"McDermott","firstName":"Jason E","initials":"JE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2961-2572"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biological Sciences Division, Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Richland, Washington, United States of America."}]}},{"fullName":"Heffron F","lastName":"Heffron","firstName":"Fred","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology and Immunology, Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, Oregon, United States of America."}]}},{"fullName":"Adkins JN","lastName":"Adkins","firstName":"Joshua N","initials":"JN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0399-0700"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biological Sciences Division, Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Richland, Washington, United States of America."}]}},{"fullName":"Cambronne ED","lastName":"Cambronne","firstName":"Eric D","initials":"ED","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3958-0463"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology and Immunology, Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, Oregon, United States of America."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0399-0700"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2961-2572"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3958-0463"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"10","journalIssueId":2318586,"dateOfPublication":"2015","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2015,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"PLoS One","title":"PloS one","essn":"1932-6203","issn":"1932-6203","nlmid":"101285081","isoabbreviation":"PLoS One"},"printPublicationDate":"2015-01-01"},"pubYear":"2015","pageInfo":"e0138466","abstractText":"The alternative sigma factor σE functions to maintain bacterial homeostasis and membrane integrity in response to extracytoplasmic stress by regulating thousands of genes both directly and indirectly. The transcriptional regulatory network governed by σE in Salmonella and E. coli has been examined using microarray, however a genome-wide analysis of σE-binding sites in Salmonella has not yet been reported. We infected macrophages with Salmonella Typhimurium over a select time course. Using chromatin immunoprecipitation followed by high-throughput DNA sequencing (ChIP-seq), 31 σE-binding sites were identified. Seventeen sites were new, which included outer membrane proteins, a quorum-sensing protein, a cell division factor, and a signal transduction modulator. The consensus sequence identified for σE in vivo binding was similar to the one previously reported, except for a conserved G and A between the -35 and -10 regions. One third of the σE-binding sites did not contain the consensus sequence, suggesting there may be alternative mechanisms by which σE modulates transcription. By dissecting direct and indirect modes of σE-mediated regulation, we found that σE activates gene expression through recognition of both canonical and reversed consensus sequence. New σE regulated genes (greA, luxS, ompA and ompX) are shown to be involved in heat shock and oxidative stress responses.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology and Immunology, Oregon Health & Science University, Portland, Oregon, United States of America.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"U01 GM094623","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"Y01 AI008401","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"Y1-AI-8401","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"GM094623","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"R21 AI088275","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Salmonella typhimurium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Salmonella Infections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bacterial Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sigma Factor","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Bacterial"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Base Sequence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Consensus Sequence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Oxidative Stress"},{"majorTopic_YN":"